==================================================
Lab 7: SQL Injection - Querying the Database Type
and Version on Oracle
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab is about using a UNION attack, the same technique learned earlier, not to steal application data this
time, but to find out basic information about the database itself, specifically which database software it is
running and what version. Knowing this is often one of the first things an attacker figures out, since different
databases have different features, functions, and table names, so the rest of the attack strategy depends on
knowing exactly what kind of database is being dealt with.

Goal: use a UNION attack to make the database reveal its own version information, directly on the product page.

==============================
WHY KNOWING THE DATABASE TYPE MATTERS

Different databases are not all the same under the hood. Oracle, MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Microsoft SQL Server
each have their own particular quirks, special tables, and built-in functions. A command or trick that works
perfectly on one database might cause an error on another. Because of this, attackers usually try to figure out
the exact database type and version early on, so they know exactly which syntax and techniques will actually
work for the rest of the attack.

=============================
STEP 1: CONFIRM THE COLUMN COUNT AND TEXT COMPATIBILITY

Just like earlier UNION attack labs, before injecting anything meaningful, we first confirm how many columns the
original query returns, and which of those columns can safely hold text. In this lab, the query happens to
return exactly two columns, and both of them are confirmed to accept text, by submitting a UNION SELECT with two
plain text values, each wrapped in quotes, and checking that both values are accepted and displayed without any
error.

Since this is an Oracle database, every SELECT statement must explicitly reference a table name, even when
selecting values that do not come from a real table. This lab uses the Oracle placeholder table called dual for
this purpose, exactly as in the walkthrough from the error-based lab earlier.

=============================
STEP 2: QUERY THE DATABASE VERSION

Oracle databases keep a special built-in view called v$version, which exists purely to store information about
the database software and its version number. This is not something the application developers created, it is
a standard part of Oracle itself, and it almost always contains a column called BANNER, which holds a readable
line of text describing the exact version running.

By replacing our earlier placeholder text values with a selection from this BANNER column instead, and keeping
the second column as NULL since only one piece of information is actually needed here, we can make the database
print its own version string directly into the page, in the same spot where a normal product would have
appeared.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

The category value sent is: a single quote, then UNION SELECT, then BANNER, comma, NULL, then FROM v$version,
then two dashes.

This tells the database to combine the original product results with a second query that pulls the version
banner text from Oracle’s built-in version view, placing that text into the first column, since that is the
column being used to display a product’s name or title on the page.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and go to a product category page, intercepting the request with Burp Suite.
Confirm the query returns two text-compatible columns, using a UNION SELECT with two placeholder text values
selected from dual.
Replace the first placeholder text with BANNER, and change the table being selected from to v$version instead
of dual.
Send the request, and observe the Oracle database version string displayed directly on the page.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests.

A session is created so cookies remain consistent, in case any later step needs them.

It asks you to paste the category filter address, and saves it as filter_url.

It builds the first payload, used purely to confirm column count and text compatibility, consisting of a single
quote, UNION SELECT, two placeholder text values in quotes separated by a comma, FROM dual, and two dashes. This
is sent as the category parameter, and the response is printed so you can visually confirm both placeholder
values show up correctly on the page without error.

It then builds the real payload used to leak the database version: a single quote, UNION SELECT, the word
BANNER, comma, NULL, FROM v$version, and two dashes. This is sent the same way, as the category parameter.

The response to this second request is searched for and printed, since the Oracle version string will appear
somewhere in the visible page content, in the same position where a normal product name would usually be.

=============================
WHY NULL IS USED FOR THE SECOND COLUMN

Since we only care about pulling out the version string, which fits inside one single column, the second column
has nothing useful to put into it. NULL is used again here, exactly like in the earlier column-counting lab,
simply as a safe placeholder that fits into any column type without causing an error.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same as every other lab, user input reaches a SQL query without being checked or
cleaned. The new idea introduced here is that a UNION attack is not limited to pulling data from an application’s
own tables, it can also be used to pull information from special, built-in system views that every database of
a certain type automatically provides, such as Oracle’s v$version.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that user input can never be interpreted as SQL structure, including attempts to
query built-in system information.

Other good practices:

Restrict database account permissions so the account used by the web application has no reason or ability to
query system views like v$version, since an application account rarely needs this kind of access for its normal
operation.
Keep database software updated, since knowing the exact version can sometimes also reveal known vulnerabilities
specific to that version, which attackers may exploit next.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

Knowing the exact database type and version is often one of the very first steps in a real SQL injection attack,
since it determines which specific functions, tables, and techniques will actually work.
Oracle requires every SELECT statement to reference a real or placeholder table, unlike some other databases
which allow a SELECT without naming any table at all.
Built-in system views like v$version exist automatically in Oracle databases and can be queried the exact same
way as any other table, once a UNION attack is possible.
This technique builds directly on the column counting and text compatibility skills learned in the very first
UNION attack labs.
